From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: kiritantakechi <kiritantakechi@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 22:15:02 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] libpfring: build against Linux 6.18.57 and later

Linux 6.18.57 took the change of proto_ops.bind's address argument to
struct sockaddr_unsized as a dependency of a Bluetooth fix, and mainline
has had it since 6.19. kmod-pf-ring does not build against those
kernels. PF_RING's development branch has the new type from 6.19.0 on,
which leaves out the stable releases that took it.

Add a patch that declares ring_bind with the new type from 6.18.57 on.

Signed-off-by: kiritantakechi <kiritantakechi@gmail.com>
Upstream-Status: Pending
---
 libs/libpfring/Makefile                       |  2 +-
 ...-with-the-address-type-of-the-kernel.patch | 29 +++++++++++++++++++
 2 files changed, 30 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)
 create mode 100644 libs/libpfring/patches/902-pf_ring-bind-with-the-address-type-of-the-kernel.patch

diff --git a/libs/libpfring/Makefile b/libs/libpfring/Makefile
index 226d0466..92003066 100644
--- a/libs/libpfring/Makefile
+++ b/libs/libpfring/Makefile
@@ -10,7 +10,7 @@ include $(INCLUDE_DIR)/kernel.mk
 
 PKG_NAME:=libpfring
 PKG_VERSION:=9.2.0
-PKG_RELEASE:=1
+PKG_RELEASE:=2
 
 PKG_SOURCE:=$(PKG_VERSION).tar.gz
 PKG_SOURCE_URL:=https://codeload.github.com/ntop/PF_RING/tar.gz/$(PKG_VERSION)?
diff --git a/libs/libpfring/patches/902-pf_ring-bind-with-the-address-type-of-the-kernel.patch b/libs/libpfring/patches/902-pf_ring-bind-with-the-address-type-of-the-kernel.patch
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..c8180c68
--- /dev/null
+++ b/libs/libpfring/patches/902-pf_ring-bind-with-the-address-type-of-the-kernel.patch
@@ -0,0 +1,29 @@
+From: kiritantakechi <kiritantakechi@gmail.com>
+Subject: [PATCH] kernel: bind with the address type of the kernel
+
+Linux 6.19 changed the address argument of proto_ops.bind from struct
+sockaddr to struct sockaddr_unsized (0e50474fa514), and 6.18.57 took
+the change as a dependency of a Bluetooth fix (946dc5835c8f). PF_RING
+9.2.0 does not build against either: GCC rejects ring_bind as an
+incompatible pointer type.
+
+Declare ring_bind with the new type from the first release that has
+it. The body stays as it is: sa_family and sa_data read the same in
+both types, and the cast to sockaddr_ll is unchanged.
+
+Signed-off-by: kiritantakechi <kiritantakechi@gmail.com>
+---
+--- a/kernel/pf_ring.c
++++ b/kernel/pf_ring.c
+@@ -5861,7 +5861,11 @@
+ /* ************************************* */
+ 
+ /* Bind to a device */
++#if(LINUX_VERSION_CODE >= KERNEL_VERSION(6,18,57))
++static int ring_bind(struct socket *sock, struct sockaddr_unsized *sa, int addr_len)
++#else
+ static int ring_bind(struct socket *sock, struct sockaddr *sa, int addr_len)
++#endif
+ {
+   struct sock *sk = sock->sk;
+   struct net *net = sock_net(sk);
